From 66d600b036c97a15de3e669addc109e422cf83db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 07:37:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10623/11171] Retarget the queued supervised run: the blocker
 is level-ups, not firings

Two conductor artifacts this hour settle what the next run should aim at, and they
overturn the game choice made an hour ago.

exp6681 proved TRANSPORT: 30 applied redirects each carry one exact live
next-outcome row, missing_receipt_count zero, explicitly with no solve claim
attached. exp6844 then audited outcome CREDIT and returned
complete_blocked_supervisor_outcome_credit_audit, its gate failing headroom_nonzero
with supervisor_effect_eligible_score 0.

So firings and plumbing are not the constraint. A redirect is only credited by a
level-up, and zero-level games produce none. The queued run was aimed at m0r0 and
sk48 precisely BECAUSE they yield zero levels -- stagnation is what makes the
supervisor fire, which was the right reasoning while the shortage was firings and is
the wrong reasoning now. Running them would add uncredited firings and reproduce the
blocked state exactly.

Retargeted to cd82 and r11l, the two games with demonstrated adapter-free level-ups
(2 each in prior e3 runs), which is what allows an arm's helped count to move.

This also refines yesterday's correction rather than reversing it. The eval harness
still collects no receipts -- that stands. But receipts DO exist from other harnesses,
so "there are none to lose" was true of the in-flight eval run and must not be read
as a claim about the ledger as a whole.

The launcher is re-armed as pid 652637. It still waits for the in-flight run to exit
and for GPU 1 to release at least 21.5 GB, and still refuses to launch rather than
fall back to the iGPU path that times out.

